---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import elevageImg from '../../../assets/img/article-insectes-elevage.webp';
---

<ArticleLayout slug="insect-meal-future-of-animal-nutrition">
  <p>
    This population growth will lead to a shortage of water, farmland and
    biodiversity for producing the animal proteins needed for livestock
    farming and human nutrition. A sustainable solution, an alternative to
    traditional animal meals (meat and fish), would be the production and use
    of insect proteins.
  </p>

  <h2>Why insects?</h2>
  <p>
    Insects have always been part of the diet of animals and of humans. Close
    to 2.5&nbsp;billion people eat them regularly, and they are an integral
    part of the diet of most wild animals:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>40% in trout,</li>
    <li>up to 50% in growing poultry, pheasants or partridges,</li>
    <li>5% in wild boar,</li>
    <li>3% in felines and canines.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Insects are rich in high-quality proteins, in fatty acids and in essential
    amino acids: they are therefore ideal candidates to replace the animal
    proteins used in particular in fish and pet food.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <Image
      src={elevageImg}
      alt="Insect farming for meal production"
      widths={[600, 900]}
      width={900}
      quality={70}
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 700px"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </figure>

  <h2>A development challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Long-term demand for insect meal in animal nutrition can be estimated at
    100,000&nbsp;tonnes per year. To meet that demand, it is important to find
    efficient large-scale farming methods. In France, their number keeps
    growing, but farmers run into certain technical and regulatory barriers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Industrial farming is energy-hungry: storing larvae in bulk causes heating
    and, since insects are ectothermic — they do not regulate their own
    temperature — the temperature of the farming premises must be controlled.
    To dissipate that heat, systems are needed that stir the mix and
    circulate air between the layers — systems that consume large amounts of
    electricity. The choice of species, control of reproduction, management of
    the raw material supply and the sizing and processing of the finished
    products will determine the viability of the production.
  </p>
  <p>The sector also faces regulatory obstacles:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      a ban on the use of proteins of animal origin in feed for farm animals,
    </li>
    <li>
      restrictions on the substrates permitted as insect feed: no manure or
      slurry, no catering waste or expired food.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Nevertheless, since 1&nbsp;July 2017, insect meal has been authorised in
    feed for farmed fish.
  </p>

  <h2>Trials carried out</h2>
  <p>
    Trials carried out with 3&nbsp;mm extruded feed containing 30%
    <i lang="la">Hermetia illucens</i> meal show a digestibility of crude
    protein and of lipids higher than that of plant meals.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table-data">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Digestibility of extruded feed containing 30% insect meal, in sea bass
        and trout, as a percentage
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Digestibility</th>
          <th scope="col">Sea bass</th>
          <th scope="col">Trout</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Crude protein</th>
          <td>91.8%</td>
          <td>87.1%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Lipids</th>
          <td>93.5%</td>
          <td>92.9%</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Insect meals therefore offer many advantages: rich in high-quality
    proteins, in fatty acids and in essential amino acids, and highly
    digestible, they allow farming that preserves natural resources. For these
    meals to become an essential component of animal feed rather than a niche
    market, price is the decisive factor: they must be able to compete with
    fishmeal in fish feed and with meat meal in pet food, the only outlets
    where they are currently authorised.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
